    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 2026 Paris Motor Show: Jeep Reveals Six-Model European Strategy for 2030, Renegade Is Set to Return\nLeo Messi Has This Yacht on Order, It’s Not Meant Entirely for Him\n6 photos\nPhoto: Sunreef\nFor one reason or another, yacht owners like to keep things private and are not very keen on being associated by name with the ships they own. From time to time, though, for unknown reasons, the identity of some yacht owners slips through the cracks, intentionally or not.\nTake, for instance, an announcement made this week by Poland-based Sunreef Yachts , a name that becomes more and more common in the industry. The makers of sailing and power yachts from the 60, 70, 80, and 100 classes revealed in their statement the name of the newest high-profile individual to order a boat.\nThat would be the Inter Miami star Leo Messi, arguably the best and most well-known soccer player in history. Shortly after the man played the last game in the jersey of the Argentine national team, it became public knowledge that Messi ordered himself a brand-new yacht.\nThat would be the Sunreef Ultima 88 catamaran that’s presently under construction at the Sunreef facility in the UAE. The ship, it seems, is not meant to be used exclusively by Messi, as it has been commissioned for the soccer player’s MIM Ocean fleet. Based in the Balearic Islands, MIM is a luxury yacht charter company born from a collab between the soccer player and Med Cat Yachts.\nThe full details of this particular Sunreef 88 have not been made public yet, but there is talk of it featuring four guest cabins, a full beam master suite, an ocean lounge that allows access to swimming, water sports, and lounging, and even a dedicated jet ski garage.\nJust like all the other 88s in the Sunreef portfolio, this one, too, will be powered by a pair of Volvo D13 engines capable of developing 1,000 horsepower each. That means the 88 can travel at top speeds of 20 knots (23 mph / 37 kph).\nIn standard configuration, the fiberglass-made multihull catamaran stands 88 feet (27 meters) long and features a 30.5 feet (9.31 meters) beam. There could be solar panels and other advanced energy solutions on deck to supply the ship’s energy needs.\nWe’ve seen another variant of the Sunreef 88 come to light earlier in September during the Cannes Yachting Festival. Called Wasabi , that one has been specced in such a way as to be able to accommodate ten people at a time and, true to its name, is equipped with a dedicated sushi chef's counter, designed not unlike the omakase tables you can see in certain Japanese restaurants.\nAs for the cost of such a yacht, it all depends on how much Messi’s desires stretch the yacht maker’s capabilities. It’s likely, though, that the starting price easily sits at around ten million dollars.\nThe Ultima 88 is part of a larger range of Sunreef yachts that also includes the 44, 55, 66, 77, 88, and the special edition 55 Brabus.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nsunreef Sunreef Yachts Leo Messi\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Not an Optical Illusion: Four Differently Sized Bentley Blowers Come Together for the First Time",
